e thought we had a  political agenda but I told him we  didn't we have nothing to do with our  members political orientation we are  politically neutral the opposition also  has a tough time in the City Council  they number six  against the front us in our 29 members  the opposition is literally getting  shouted down by the majority the phone  arsenal is using in as a showcase and  has brought in its top talents it's very  difficult to stand our ground in such  circumstances and convince anybody to  vote for us this is a real war machine  the front as soon as members say they  are just defending themselves against a  very aggressive opposition  [Music]  India is a magnet for African students  thousands many from Nigeria and Sudan  study there but attacks last month  against African students near Delhi have  cast a shadow we have this report Abdul  Rahim machmood came to India three years  ago from Nigeria to study Business  Administration he was drawn by the  country's reputation for a low-cost high  quality education but the recent mob  attack against African students near  this private university close to Delhi  has left him and his friends shaken  Abdul Rahim who lives off campus says he  stayed indoors for a whole week after  the attack fearing further violence I  have to be looking over my shoulders  I don't feel secure this itching is  dangerous out there  foreign students account for a large  chunk of the private universities  revenue authorities are alarmed the  recent racist attacks could threaten its  funding and tarnish India's image as a  global education hub definitely we are  worried that the fewer students might  come because of this incident and and we  have to do our damage control in the  terms of we have to introspect we have  to see how it has happened why it has  happened the education sector is a big  part of India's growing ties with Africa  experts say wooing African students is a  way for Delhi to build on its soft power  and help expand India's bilateral trade  and economic footprint in the continent  today we have a situation where India is  engaged in a certain amount of bilateral  trade with African countries India  exports a fair amount to African  countries and it is importing the  equivalent of primary material primary  resources as for instance in the  hydrocarbon sector now if we have a  situation where the average African  student is unsafe my sense is that  Indian investment in Africa as also  Indian exports to Africa would be  adversely impacted abdul rahim has a few  months to go before he finishes his  studies and heads back  he says the attack and his experience in  India has left him disillusioned and  determined not to come back any African  that will that is considering to come to  India will have to think twice now  because of this incident so this is the  reality it has really affected our  relationship with the with India India  has its work cut out to convince  international students that it's still a  safe bet  for people smugglers the border fence  between the US and Mexico is good for  business but for the families / it the  barrier stands for painful separation  [Music]  after a long separation a tender reunion  at the border fence era clips in the  United States his mother in Mexico  he can't visit her she has no visa this  is the only way they can see each other  in person I can I crash in Mexico so  since seven months having seen her and  today I got a day to see her I feel  really good but I don't know why doncha  wants to make a while there's a lot of  families here in sukhasana Nogales they  don't cross and there's only place to  see the parents or the family Sheriff  Tony Estrada sees these types of scenes  daily he was born in Nogales Mexico but  grew up on the US side of the border in  Nogales Arizona he says the two cities  share close ties and is skeptical of a  border war obviously a big concrete wall  that maybe 20 30 feet high it's not  something we're really looking forward  to you know it just continued to  separate us more and more and besides  that it's not the answer in recent years  millions of dollars have been spent on  enhancing border security but hundreds  of thousands still tried to make the  crossing every year here in Arizona most  travel through deserts and canyons  breakfast in a migrant shelter on the  other side of the border most of the  people here were just deported from the  US this morning after trying to cross  the border illegally they come from  Mexico Guatemala or Honduras they risk  everything and paid smugglers large sums  of money now they are not sure what will  happen to them I have three or four  family members there that I've never met  so I'd like to meet them I crossed the  border with a smuggler left me in the  desert then the u.s. Border Patrol found  and arrested me I was held for twenty  days and today they brought me here  father Shankar all hears these kinds of  stories again and again the Jesuit  priest is in charge of the migrant  center day after day he sees traumatized  people Carol says the drug cartels are  firmly in control of the people  smuggling here to change that they need  more than just a border war so there  really needs to be serious investment in  economic development I would say  especially in southern Mexico and in the  northern part of Central America so that  people can have a dignified life where  they live and they don't feel like  they're forced to migrate to leave their  homes to find a better life for now he  and our volunteers tried to help the  people here as best they can with advice  medicine and clothing he knows most will  try again to reach the United States  with a hope of a better life  our last report takes us to Berlin not  where you would expect to find a rather  modest Detroit clapboard house once home  to a famous human rights activist but  you'd be wrong  [Music]  2672 South Deacon Street Detroit this  was the house where Rosa Parks lived  after fleeing Alabama from Michigan only  it's not in Michigan anymore it's here  in the German capital when no one in the  u.s. showed interest in keeping it  standing the city of Detroit sold it for  $500 the fact that they sold it for $500  the American government thought that  this house was worth $500 it speaks  volumes it's actually evidence of an  enormous neglect New York native Ryan  Mendoza brought the house to Berlin  where it now stands in the district of  vetting following a long journey Rosa  Parks is nice Rhea McCauley seen here on  the left gave the house to Mendoza as a  gift last summer he had the house  dismantled and shipped to Berlin where  it now stands on his property  Fabian Mendoza who helped her husband  reassemble the house says the story of  Rosa Parks reflects the struggles of  many in her circumstance they went noise  because with the hope that they find a  better life and if it's an employment  that they can live the couple put up the  funds to transport the house to Berlin  where they were set on having it  reassembled as an exhibition the artists  hope that the house will eventually be  returned to the United States and stand  as a National Monument perhaps in one of  the gardens at the White House but for  now the spirit of Rosa Parks is present  in Berlin  [Music]  you
 

